-- The first program the rewritten resolve stage compiled end to end - Analysis-IR.md part 11.4,
-- milestone 1. A module-level constant, and a `main` that hands it back.
--
-- Deliberately free of everything: no import, no prelude name, no `Int`. `compiler/resolve_mir`
-- does not build Core yet, and the width names are builtin rather than declared - `Int` and
-- `Long` are Core aliases over `I32` and `I64`, so a fixture that writes the width directly
-- needs no standard library at all.
--
-- It is an ordinary fixture as well: it compiles clean, and the `.run.expect` beside it is what
-- every fixture that writes a width this way is checked against - the same file, because one
-- language has one answer to give, whichever program asks, which is the point of writing the
-- width directly instead of importing `Int`.

let answer = 42 :: I32

fn main() -> I32 = answer
